SamIAm wrote:
>
> ... what comes to mind, regarding
> this argument of not being similar to a "graded or "advancing" manner
> of gameplay, is this:  the key to the nature of Nethack is the RNG. In
> essence, it's more like a game of poker than a game of chess,

Far more so than poker.  In poker you have a small number of
rolls.  In Nethack you end up getting dozens of rolls per game.
Note that the higher the number of rolls the more like the real
world - quantum mechanics does rolls constantly on a subatomic
level.  It would take many orders of magnitude for Nethack to
be "like" real life, but it is always several orders of magnitude
further along that path than poker.

In a sense the RNG creates free will within the game.

> because
> in chess the variables are always precisely the same, and you can then
> improve your skills in manipulating those variables.  Whereas in NH,
> even if you do develop skills in combat technique, inventory
> management, scroll/spell usage, it still comes down to the cards you
> are dealt, so to speak.

What happens in any one roll comes down to the cards, but the
game involves thousands of rolls.  In poker you can lose any one
hand so you have to use strategy to average out to end up with a
winning night.  In Nethack you can lose any one *swing* so you
have to use strategy to average to to end up with a surviving
*level*.

> A skillful payer can play the odds for a
> winning hand in poker, but not when they're dealt rubbish to start.
> However, with bluffing and attentiveness, victory can still be had.

Not the Nethack model.  It isn't random enough for that.  Just
like the real world doesn't appear random because there are so
many random events, games average out and Nethack is
farther along that curve than poket.

> I don't know what this all has to do with the arument for/against
> savescumming, except to say that in a game of poker you can't "do
> over" a bad play!

Savescumming preserves context at one turn.  How do you
really know that your mistake here and now is your real mistake?
Many times the mistake was several levels before, something
like enhancing a melee class instead of a missile or spell class,
but no amount of savescumming is ever going to teach you that.
That's the real problem with svavescumming - It can teach
tactics, but Nethack is NOT a tactical game.  It also leads the
savescummer to view Nethack as a tactical game but since it
is a strategic game no amount of effort at improving tactics helps
enough.

> But I still find odd, awkward, and just a little
