> players defend the "one strike and you're out" gameplay.  Perhaps it's
> supposed to mirror the harsh reality of "real life", for all of us who
> lack real risk in our lives ;^)?

Nope.  Here's the important difference - People play the typical
graphical video game for maybe a year then get bored with it.  People
play nethack for over a decades without getting bored with it.  How
is such a difference possible?  The only way to stay interesting for
over a decade is to be unforgiving on some levels and forgiving on
other levels.  It's just that the stuff that can be learned from
savescumming aren't the lessons that can keep the game interesting.
I've tried it that way and learned my lesson.  I figure you'll have to
use savescumming for a year or so and discover it isn't working any
more.  Because it is incapable of teaching the parts of the game that
are interesting.



